goal:
The course is much an invitation as an introduction to understanding the “political” and how it harbours the good life and fosters coexistence. While introductions map out a territory and lay the groundwork for more detailed research, invitations invite students to:
1) critically assess key social and political ideas and concepts, both orally and in writing;
2) appreciate the nuances of persuasive and sophisticated language in debates about politics, society, the economy and who we are more generally;
3) develop an ability in scrutinising to what extent theories enable us to understand and explain facts and values, instead of simply learning the facts and values themselves;
4) and, finally, broaden their perspective on many of today’s political problems, together with their potential resolution.

The purpose of the course is for students to:
1) read critically by asking pertinent questions and evaluating ideas in a text;
2) recognise core assumptions and wider implications of a text;
3) read texts of different genres and disciplines in the humanities and social sciences.
4) ascertain the meaning of a passage by identifying evidence and narrative sequences;
5) recognise the implicit assumptions and values underlying a written work;
6) assess ideas in terms of the rhetorical structures used to persuade readers;
7) distinguish between facts and opinions in a text;
8) identify the tone, mood and voice of a text through an analysis of its linguistic features, context of production and literary devices.

Participants will:
1. Gain a solid knowledge of central authors, texts, and themes of Edo period literature;
2. Be able to identify and contextualise the characteristics and structures of various genres of Edo period literature;
3. Be able to articulate their interpretation of Edo period literature using appropriate terminology;
4. Cultivate skills to critically examine and reflect on the social, political, economic, religious, and scientific contexts of the Edo period.
